, "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-25T20:07:50.670Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPZG7wYLjfaYTSkTCeZp", "thinking_duration_ms": 107754}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPZG8CubHz2GXV4SptR6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Answer: candidate 3.** It is the daily plan whose orders track mean demand most closely. My confidence is low (p ≈ 0.42). Candidate 7 is a close second, because the difference is within the error of my rough estimate.\n\n**Order sizes.** I read each plan as a multiple of the forecast for the day its order arrives.\n\n| Candidate | Order type | Size vs. forecast |\n|---|---|---|\n| 7 | Daily | ×1.00 |\n| 3 | Daily | ×1.05 |\n| 0 | Daily | ×1.10 |\n| 6 | Daily | ×1.16, cut to ×1.00 for the last two orders |\n| 1 | Daily | ×1.20 |\n| 5 | Daily | ×1.30 |\n| 2 | One order every 3 days | ×1.10 |\n| 4 | One order every 2 days | ×1.10 |\n\nMean demand is about ×1.046 of the forecast, so candidate 3 sits almost exactly on it.\n\n**Losses every plan shares.**\n- Product 0 runs out on days 1–3, about 105 units of shortage, because its first delivery can only arrive on day 4.\n- Product 1 runs short by about 23 units on day 1.\n- Cash never limits any order, and warehouse space is not a constraint for the daily plans.\n\n**Why the order size matters.** None of the plans reacts to actual demand. So stock behaves like a random walk that stops at zero, with a daily spread of about 14 units for product 0 and about 12 for product 1. Over the 26–28 days, each extra unit ordered either:\n- prevents a lost sale: +15 for product 0, +12 for product 1; or\n- piles up: you pay storage for it, and anything left at the end is wasted (−12 for product 0, −5 for product 1).\n\n**Estimated losses** (lost sales + leftover stock + storage), from a continuous random-walk approximation:\n\n| Size vs. forecast | Product 0 | Product 1 | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ×1.00 | ≈2280 | ≈1440 | ≈3720 |\n| ×1.046 | ≈2330 | ≈1335 | ≈3665 |\n| ×1.06 | ≈2380 | ≈1325 | ≈3705 |\n| ×1.10 | ≈2720 | ≈1450 | ≈4170 |\n| ×1.16 | ≈3780 | ≈1960 | ≈5740 (≈5500 with candidate 6's cut at the end) |\n\nThe best size is around the mean, ×1.03–1.06, which is candidate 3. Product 0 leans toward slightly smaller orders and product 1 toward slightly larger ones.\n\n**Batch plans (2 and 4).** Ordering every 2 or 3 days saves about 18–25 per day in the fixed 37 fee. But the stock held between deliveries costs about 27–55 per day in storage, so batching loses overall. Both also order at ×1.10, which is already too much.\n\n**Under random demand,** plans near ×1.05 are the most robust. Ordering less risks lost sales, which cost more than leftovers. Ordering more steadily builds unsold stock that ends up wasted."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3882, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2304, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3882}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-25T20:08:01.012Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPZG7wYLjfaYTSkTCeZp"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "